Carl N Mummert wrote:
> 
> Why not add a few more bigroom variants, with chasms (made of air)
> ...  Brief testing shows that ... you can apparantly walk on air...

	That behaviour is inherited from the Plane of Air.  We
either need a new terrain type, "chasm", or we need to change the
behaviour of "air" terrain when you aren't on the plane of air.
Thinking aloud:

	As a symbol for a huge hole in the ground, "air" terrain 
looks funny.  Tiles show it as blue sky; I believe that it looks 
like solid rock in TTY.

	"Holes in the ground" are not acceptable as a faked-up 
"chasm" terrain.  It's possible to walk through a square with a
hole in the ground without falling.  Chasms wouldn't allow that.

	OK, we would need a new terrain type, "chasm".  The tile for
it shouldn't be solid black, so you can tell that it isn't rock.
Make it gray or brown, or black with a couple of off-color pixels.
(Or, if you're full of energy, do a complete set of tiles for the
edges of chasms.)  For TTY, look for a symbol not already in use.  
Use the trap symbol as a last resort.

	Behaviour:  

   o  If you step on a chasm square while not levitating or flying,
      "You step into the chasm.  You fall..."  When levitating or
      flying, you can do a controlled descent.

   o  If the next level down is a special level (= it has its own
      non-random map), then your X-Y location on the map is pre-
      served.  You do *not* end up in a random location.  (Reason-
      ing:  every "chasm" level will be a special level with a non-
      random map.  Keeping the map location unchanged lets the level
      designers have special effects, like perfectly vertical
      chasms extending for several levels, or guaranteed soft
      landings, or guaranteed soft landings in lava.)

   o  For a controlled descent, you go to the next level down.

   o  For an uncontrolled descent:

     -  On the next level down, you're shown however much of the map
        is visible.

     -  "You're falling!  There is [monster name, or terrain type if
        there's no monster] below you.  You have a second to do some-
        thing, or to steer a bit."  You can do one action.  Direction
        keys steer you.  A lot of actions are disallowed (engraving;  
        pickaxing into the wall beside you) or changed (moving onto
        the same square as a monster is not an attack:  you land on
        the monster).

     -  When you hit, you take (number of levels fallen) * (5 + (weight
        carried/100)) d10 damage.  You likely die if you're low level
        or if you fall more than one level.  Damage multipliers:
        (take the first one that applies)
          0.25  water
          0.50  lava, gas spore, engulfer (and you are engulfed), or
                any "b", "j", or "P"  
          0.75  non-tiny monsters
          1.50  spiked pit

     -  Landing on a monster:  non-tiny monsters take half of n * 
        (5 + w/100) D10 damage.  Tiny monsters die.  Landing on a
        footrice, green slime, or monster with a corroding or rotting
        attack gives a successful stoning / sliming / armor damaging
        hit.  Ditto their corpses.  Landing on a pet scares the pet.

   o  Non-flying pets will not jump into chasms after you.  Non-
      flying steeds refuse to jump into chasms.

   o  If you're levitating or flying and the level above has a chasm, 
      you can go up a level.  If the square you're on isn't directly
      below a chasm square, you end up on a random chasm square.

   o  Scrolls of earth fill in chasm squares, but if they don't
      attach to the edge of the chasm, the new ground crumbles to
      boulders, which fall.  Blessed scrolls put ground under you if
      you're flying or levitating.

   o  Failed untrapping of webs can't put webs into chasm squares.
      Rolling and thrown boulders fall.  Created monsters fall.

   o  Boulders going into chasms become piles of rock if they hit
      normal ground on the Nth level down.  They fill in water 
      squares 90% of the time and lava 10% of the time, just like
      normal.  They can hit monsters or not, as convenient to the
      coder:  the player will never be on the level when a boulder
      hits, so it's not of central importance.

   o  Digging a hole beside a chasm square gives a chasm square.  (I
      worried about lunatics making an entire level into a chasm, but
      the staircase in would be safe.  And if magic portals can be 
      destroyed by digging, then this is already a problem....)  If
      this leaves a disconnected piece of floor, that piece crumbles
      into boulders and falls into the chasm.

   o  Creating a water square beside a chasm fails:  the water drains
      into the chasm.

   o  Technical detail:  the level compiler for room-type levels
      doesn't have to know how to deal with "chasm" terrain.  As long
      as there's a unique symbol for chasms on maze-type levels, we're
      OK.


	Ugh.  It would certainly be different, but it would need a 
lot of coding. 

